<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dutch East India Company</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as VOC, Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> (VOC) was a chartered trading company founded in 1602, with powers to wage war, make treaties and govern territory in Asia. It was the main instrument of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>, displaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> from much of the spice trade and was a model and rival for the English East India Company of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company.jpg" alt="Dutch East India Company" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_East_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Merchant communities lived in their own quarters around the city: Chinese, Mon, Malays, Persians, Japanese, Portuguese, and later the <b>Dutch East India Company</b>, which opened a trading post in the early seventeenth century, followed by the English and the French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's early decades were marked by intense commercial rivalry with the Dutch Republic, whose East India Company (<b>VOC</b>) long outperformed its English counterpart, and by the gradual development of the fiscal-military state that would underpin British global power: the Navigation Acts (from 1651), the Bank of England…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The British Cape Colony was built on the settlement founded at Table Bay by the <b>Dutch East India Company</b> (VOC) in 1652.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch East Indies were created when the Dutch state took over the Asian possessions of the bankrupt <b>Dutch East India Company</b> (VOC), whose charter expired at the end of 1799.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hugo Grotius wrote Mare Liberum (1609) in defence of the <b>VOC</b>, and it became a founding text of international law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Dutch East India Company</b> (VOC), founded in 1602, and the Dutch West India Company, founded in 1621, extended this commerce to Asia, Africa and the Americas, including the Atlantic slave trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
